+### A player limited to one picture a frame falls behind its sound
+
+- **Symptom:** a movie's sound runs ahead of its picture and ends before it, so the movie's later scenes play in silence: the log's `audren: renderer 0 rendered N frames, M of them not silent` shows the mix silent for up to a minute or more, with no underrun and no red line, and the movie's decoder reaches `end of stream` long after the movie's length. Seen where frames take two vsyncs during the movie (a slower GPU, a busy Mac) and, every time, with the frame rate set to 30. The frame rate and the game's speed read right.
+- **Cause:** the game's player, not the decoder. Ocarina of Time's (in `main`, v131072) adds each frame's time to a count of pictures due (`main+0x3b5ca60`: the time object's frame time × 60, at the movie's rate) and advances that many (`main+0x3b61ea0` pops them from its queue and shows the last), but at most the limit its constructor set (`main+0x3b5cb2c`); what is left stays in the count. The constructor (`main+0x3b5cec0`) sets the limit to 1, or to 2 for a last argument of 2 or more, and the queue to twice the limit; the game's one caller passes 1. The console holds one vsync a frame in its movies. Here the [frame step](frame-rate.md#a-fixed-frame-step-plays-in-slow-motion-on-a-slow-mac) hands the game steps of two and three vsyncs: the count asks for two pictures, one is taken, and the picture is behind for the rest of the movie, while the movie's sound, a stream of the game's sound engine, keeps real time. Read in play (`DM_PROBE=main:3b5cb28` with `DM_PROBE_ALL=1`: `x8` the pictures due, `x9` the limit): the limit 1 and the count at 1,350 pictures after 45 s at a 30 fps frame step.
+- **Fix:** a function-matched cut in the profile ([`patches.py`](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/patches.py), `MATCH_CUTS`): the constructor takes its own value for 2 whatever its caller passes (limit 2, queue 4), so a frame of two vsyncs shows every second picture and a movie that fell behind catches up at two pictures a frame. It is a change of the game's code because the limit is the game's: the host's decoder gives every picture it is asked for.
+- **Check:** a host with one log line per 60 pictures (stream time against host time) and per 200 renderer frames (how many not silent), headless, `DM_FPS_TARGET=30`, `DM_KEYS=50:A~0.2` from an empty save (the title, then the opening's first movie: 9,254 pictures at 60 a second, 154.2 s).
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md#frame-rate) ([issue 1501](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/1501)), numbers in its note.
+- **Open:** frames held three vsyncs still lose a picture each until shorter frames catch up: 3 is no value of the constructor's, and with it forced the game stopped at the movie's start with a copy to a null pointer in `sdk` (cause not looked into). Other games of this engine were not looked at.
